<p>Well, hello again! I don’t think that I have ever had a gap in posting as long as this before. Several months ago, I decided to explore moving this blog from both my previous host (<a href="https://netlify.com">Netlify</a>), and away from the static site generator I had been using (<a href="https://gohugo.io">Hugo</a>, more on those decisions below). However, life got in the way. We sadly lost a family member, Mr. Bsag got an injury that resulted in us spending 9 hours overnight in A&amp;E (the Emergency Room, for North Americans), I had some bad bouts of ME/CFS fatigue, and so on. In between those times, I started to chip away at the blog moving task, but I kept getting delayed by having to make decisions about a long chain of dependencies. If I moved to Host A with static site generator B, would I be able to deploy from git host Z?</p><p>You might be thinking, “but surely your old site was still working fine all this time, so nothing (apart from the ‘life’ stuff) was preventing you from posting here in the meantime?”. That would be technically correct, gentle and perceptive reader, but it does not take into account my stubborn decision to only publish any new articles once the migration was complete. I know, it makes no sense. Anyway, the bulk of the work is done now, and the remaining tweaks and improvements can wait until later. I have a few ideas for posts that have stacked up, but for now, I’ll go through the decisions behind the move and what was involved before I forget why I put myself through all this.</p><h3>Hosting: Netlify to Bunny.net</h3><p>It all started with a few niggles and ‘back of the mind’ worries. Netlify had been very good to me as a free (as in beer) host for many years. However, there have been increasing indications that Netlify is growing way beyond the hobbyist market. The dashboard and configuration interface has become much more complex, and everything is organised around your ‘team’, which in my case is just me. The free tier is still there, but ‘grandfathered in’, which is often a harbinger of it disappearing altogether at a later stage, or becoming much more limited. Netlify have reassured users that it will be there forever, and I have no particular reason to doubt them, but I have experienced that transition too many times to avoid all cynicism about it. I could have just moved up to their ‘Personal’ paid tier at $9 per month, but I still would not have escaped the complexity or the feeling that the service is not really targeted at people like me (i.e. ‘small web’ folk). The other issue was that I wanted to move to an EU-based host.</p><p>I found some good alternatives for hosting. <a href="https://www.nearlyfreespeech.net">NearlyFreeSpeech.net</a> (recommended by <a href="https://neatnik.net">Adam</a>) looked great, though I might have needed a bit of wrangling to run a static site there. It is USA-based though, so that one was out. <a href="https://www.statichost.eu">Statichost.eu</a> also looked fantastic and is obviously 100% European. They are static host specialists, have a free ‘Hobby’ tier and a €9 per month ‘Starter’ tier (at time of writing), either of which would have worked well for my blog. Their documentation is also excellent: I read through almost all of it in less than an hour, and it is extremely clear and easy to follow. It was a very close competition between them and <a href="https://bunny.net">Bunny.net</a>, but in the end the latter won by a nose.</p><p>I have to admit that, even though I had heard many positive things about Bunny.net, it intimidated me. I think the terminology that I hadn’t encountered before (‘pull zones’ and ‘storage zones’) put me off, but <a href="https://burgeonlab.com/blog/hosting-static-sites-with-github-pages-or-bunny-net/">Naty’s write up</a> about using it (with Hugo) helped a lot to demystify it. When I set up an account and poked around, I found it delightfully easy to understand for the most part. Bunny combines storage in their storage zones (which can be S3 compatible if that matters to you) with a content delivery network (CDN) via their pull zones to get those files to people anywhere in the world as quickly as possible. The pull zone gets pointed at either an external URL (if you store your files elsewhere), or at a Bunny storage zone, and you tell Bunny what domain name you want to associate with the pull zone. When you set up the storage zone, you pick which geographical locations you would like a ‘node’ to be located in, so that visitors in that region get the pages as quickly as possible. Each one adds cost, and you can only add not subtract them, so it’s better to start with two or three and see how things go. I selected three: UK, Germany and East coast USA for mine.</p><p>Bunny’s pricing also attracted me, because you pay-as-you-go for resource usage. I will find out over time what I end up paying, but for a fairly low traffic, static site, I think it is unlikely to cost me more than Statichost.eu, and probably considerably less. It’s reassuring that you can set bandwidth limits so that if you get DDoS-ed (or Daring Fireballed), you won’t end up paying more than you intended. However, they also have a free ‘Bunny Shield’ service that analyses your logs and can turn on smart protection against attacks, DDoS and so on. I also like the fact that Bunny has other things I can try over time, like edge scripting or using databases if I want to bring more interactive elements into the site, and it will generally only cost me a small incremental price in line with my usage.</p><p>Finally, one thing that I <a href="https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/haver">havered</a> a bit over was how I was actually going to deploy my site to my new host. Netlify had a built-in facility to link up a GitHub repository so that committing to the main branch of the repo would trigger a build. It worked fine, but I am also trying to move away from GitHub (because of their poor uptime and Copilot AI infestation), so I had to consider whether I could recreate that at Codeberg or my own private self-hosted Forgejo instance. There were difficulties with both, because Codeberg frowns upon private repositories and projects that are not proper Open Source projects useful to others, for very good and understandable reasons. My own private Forgejo repository on the other hand sits behind a Tailscale VPN so that only people on my ‘Tailnet’ (i.e. only me 😄) can access it. There is probably a way around it, but I expect that it would make integrating with Bunny (or other hosts) tricky. This was actually one of the main sticking points when I was thinking about dependencies.</p><p>It turns out that there was a very simple solution. Bunny has a CLI tool (called <code>bunny</code> naturally enough), which lets you do a bunch of things via a terminal to interact with your account, including sites (i.e. pull zones). This tool is still in Beta, so there is little documentation about it on the site yet, but using the built-in help sets you up with the basics. Essentially, you can use <code>bunny login</code> to authenticate via a browser, then <code>bunny sites create [my-site-name]</code> to create your site and link it with a pull zone. After that, <code>bunny sites deploy [./dir]</code> uploads your built static site to your storage zone as a preview. Onec you have checked it, you can publish it with <code>bunny sites deployments publish [deploy-id]</code>. I found that it even automatically detected that I was running Eleventy (of which more in a moment), and asked me if I wanted to build it first! It’s a really nice tool which gives you plenty of feedback about options and progress along the way. Incidentally, this also means that there is no need to pay for any build time as there would be when pushing a commit in a CI action from a git forge. All the building happens locally, and then it just transfers a bunch of the resulting files.</p><h3>Static site generator: Hugo to Eleventy (11ty)</h3><p>So what was my problem with <a href="https://gohugo.io">Hugo</a>? Not much, in truth. It is still one of the fastest out there, rendering all 1500+ pages of my blog in under a second. I also liked that it was a single static binary, so you didn’t have to maintain a complicated tool chain with a lot of dependencies. However, the templating language has always been a bit difficult to follow, and — particularly over recent years — there has been an increase in the rate of development, with several breaking changes. I felt anxious every time I had to go in and fix things, and it didn’t motivate me to try to tweak some of the things that bothered me.</p><p>Again, I looked around at other options. <a href="https://www.getzola.org/documentation/getting-started/overview/">Zola</a> would have been an easy option as it is a simpler static site generator (written in Rust), but structurally similar to Hugo, so that any changes I might need to make would be fairly minimal. In the end, I was swayed by all the cool people using <a href="https://www.11ty.dev">11ty</a>, so I pulled on my Learning Trousers and worked though the excellent <a href="https://learneleventy.dev/">Learn Eleventy</a>. That set me up well with the basics (despite the fact that I am a rank beginner, if that, in Javascript). My approach was then to remove the details from that tutorial site, adapt my Hugo templates to the Nunjucks template format, and test the site with a handful of Markdown format content posts copied over from my Hugo blog. Thank goodness for Markdown with Yaml front matter, because I had to make remarkably few changes to the actual posts to get 11ty to build them without error.</p><p>The main sticking points were shortcodes to embed GitHub Gists (thankfully very few of those), and shortcodes (or various random bits of other markup) for images within the content. The gists were easily dealt with, but the images issue needed more thought. Most of my images in recent years have been stored on Backblaze B2, then converted and optimised on the fly with Netlify’s optimiser. Bunny.net also has an image optimiser (at additional monthly cost), and so I tried it out. It worked fine, but I realised that it would be much simpler to use 11ty’s built-in image optimisation to store the images alongside the content in my Bunny storage zone, then get 11ty to create optimised/resized versions at build time. This should speed up page loading, and also make better use of caching. It might also encourage me to post more articles with images, as the friction of having to remember how to upload images to Backblaze has often made me think an article can do without an image, out of sheer laziness!</p><p>Wrangling 11ty’s image optimisation took a bit of time, but once you understand how it works, it’s really magical. Dealing with the legacy hotchpotch of image markup in my old posts was rather less magical. By this point, I had copied across all my old posts from Hugo to see how 11ty fared with my archives. I went through a similar thing when I moved to Hugo in (<em>checks archives</em>) 2016 — wow, 10 years ago. While Hugo had a <em>lassez-faire</em> approach to handling broken image links and other markup encrustations, waiving them through airily, 11ty issued a firm ‘no’. What I ended up doing was running the command to serve the site, looking at the error, fixing the error with that post, and repeating <em>endlessly</em>. Some bits of code just had to go in the end. I went through a phase of using images of albums, books and films from Amazon to illustrate my reviews, but a) all those links are broken now and b) I don’t want to have anything more to do with Amazon than is strictly necessary. For those, I just got rid of the image and left my review. I also had a few photos that had ‘moblogged’ (anyone else remember that phase?), but which didn’t make it to Backblaze, and I can’t find the originals of. If the text made any kind of sense without the image, I just removed the image, and if not, I removed the post. It was a real process, but eventually, the site built without error! I’ll probably write more about 11ty later on, but overall, I am really impressed with its flexibility. It is slower to build than Hugo, but it is doing the image optimisation upfront, so it’s not really a problem. The development server runs locally in about 2-3 seconds, and a full clean build (including generating the images) takes about 30 seconds. Given that it’s my time and computing resources that it’s using (and not metred build minutes), I’m fine with that.</p><p>Apart from this article, changes to the links in the footer, and an improved search facility (<a href="https://www.rousette.org.uk/archives/">take a look at it here</a>), there is very little else that has changed structurally or visually. I made the decision to move the RSS feed to <code>feed.xml</code> instead of <code>index.xml</code>, as the former is the much more common pattern, and <code>index.xml</code> is just weird. I have set up a 301 redirect from the old to the new feed so your feed reader should pick it up, but if you subscribe and are not seeing new posts, you may need to edit the feed url to the new one.</p><p>There will be more changes to come though: I’ve got some internal changes planned to speed up the site even more, and some long-overdue functional and visual tweaks to make.</p><h3>DNS provider: Netlify to omg.lol’s own DNS Kitchen!</h3><p>We’re nearly there. If you see this post at all, my DNS tinkering worked. My DNS for this site was hosted at Netlify, so I also needed to move that in moving away from their service. Luckily for me, <a href="https://neatnik.net">Adam Newbold</a>, the genius behind <a href="https://omg.lol">omg.lol</a> that I use for an increasing number of my online services, has been building <a href="https://dns.kitchen">DNS Kitchen</a>. I recently got a lifetime address at omg.lol, which entitled me to try all the exciting works in progress, including DNS Kitchen, which isn’t otherwise open to sign ups.</p><p>I asked Adam if that was ok, and he agreed enthusiastically, even after finding out that I’m scared of DNS. Only the super-expert mode for creating zone files is fully built out at the moment, but Adam held my hand at every stage, and was incredibly patient with my (many) questions. I learned so much in the process, and his explanations have massively demystified DNS for me. I still don’t really understand what determines when the old records have been fully flushed down the drains everywhere, and the bath filled up with the fresh, new DNS records (I think that’s how it works?), but I feel so much more confident about writing my zone file by hand from now on. Thanks Adam!</p><p>That’s all for now, but there will be more coming soon, I promise.</p>